- Story & Context: Squatting alone on the tar-paper roof of the compressor shed behind Shaft Four, disgraced winchman Tam Cleary peered through a cracked pair of Soviet binoculars at the jagged W-shape of stars, tasting the bitter copper of blood and battery acid on his tongue. Having severed two fingers during the wildcat strike when he sabotaged the shaft-hoist to trap the scab managers underground, he plugged his modified chanter directly into an overdriven car stereo, playing a weeping, half-strangled lament to drown his confession. With the grease-coated iron air vents beside him vibrating in sympathetic fury against the night wind, his gravelly voice rasped out into the cassette deck: "I dug two miles down just to realize I hate every star that looked down while we choked."
